# Safe Python execution and I/O

Created: 2026-11-04T21:45:23Z (first recorded addition in Git)
Last updated: 2026-11-10T15:55:25Z

Apply these practices to project Python code using the capabilities below.
Shared security requirements are defined in [security.mdc](security.mdc).
Coursework follows its applicable course instructions.

## External programs

Prefer suitable existing Python APIs when they meet the task's needs.

When invoking a program, use an argument list with shell interpretation disabled.
Do not interpolate external input into shell commands.

Validate arguments that select executables, options, paths, or sensitive behavior.
An argument list prevents shell interpretation; it does not prevent a program
from interpreting an external value as an option or performing an unsafe operation.

Handle nonzero exit status explicitly, using `check=True` or deliberate return-code
handling. Set appropriate timeouts when a process could stall the operation.

Choose the working directory and inherited environment deliberately.
Avoid passing secrets through command-line arguments.

## Parsing and deserialization

Do not use `eval` or `exec` to interpret external or configurable data.

Do not load untrusted data through pickle, shelve, marshal, or other mechanisms
that can execute code or reconstruct unsafe objects.

Use a parser appropriate to the required format:

- JSON parsing for JSON.
- Safe-loading facilities for YAML.
- XML parsing configured to prevent unsafe entity and external-resource handling.

Validate the parsed structure and relevant values before use.

Do not assume a data-only parser makes unlimited input safe. Apply relevant
input-size and complexity bounds. `ast.literal_eval` is not a general-purpose
safe parser for arbitrary untrusted input.

Introduce additional parsing libraries through [dependencies.mdc](dependencies.mdc)
when the format and threat boundary justify them.

## Filesystem access

Use `pathlib` or suitable filesystem APIs rather than constructing shell commands.

For externally supplied paths, establish the permitted base or resource scope.
Resolve and validate paths using path-aware operations, accounting for absolute
paths, parent traversal, and symlinks where relevant.

Do not rely on string-prefix comparisons to establish path containment.
Resolution followed by a containment check is not sufficient isolation when
another actor can change filesystem entries between validation and access.
Use stronger filesystem or operating-system controls when that boundary requires it.

Validate archive extraction destinations and links before extraction.
Use appropriate library safeguards and bounds for extracted size and file count.

Use temporary-file APIs rather than predictable filenames in shared temporary
directories.

Follow [pipeline_io.mdc](pipeline_io.mdc) for destinations, preservation,
overwrites, retained stages, and cleanup.

